WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.969
Understanding macOS layout gives investigators a map for likely evidence locations.

00:00:05.969 --> 00:00:12.894
Applications are often installed under /Applications, but user-specific application data usually lives elsewhere.

00:00:12.894 --> 00:00:25.549
User home directories are under /Users, and they may contain documents, downloads, desktop files, browser data, shell history, application traces, and a user-specific Library directory.

00:00:25.549 --> 00:00:32.473
That user Library is one of the richest evidence areas on a Mac.

00:00:32.473 --> 00:00:40.353
/Library contains system-wide application support data, launch items, logs, preferences, and configuration shared across users.

00:00:40.353 --> 00:00:51.097
~/Library contains user-specific preferences, caches, saved application state, browser and productivity app artifacts, application support files, containers, and recent activity traces.

00:00:51.097 --> 00:01:02.320
The distinction between system Library and user Library matters because it helps separate device-wide configuration from activity tied to a specific profile.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:10.677
/System contains operating system components and is protected on modern macOS versions by platform security features.

00:01:10.677 --> 00:01:17.124
/private/var can contain logs, temporary data, databases, spool files, and system state.

00:01:17.124 --> 00:01:26.436
Temporary directories and caches can be useful, but they may be volatile and heavily affected by normal operation.

00:01:26.436 --> 00:01:33.838
Analysts should understand what is persistent, what is user-specific, and what may change quickly.

00:01:33.838 --> 00:01:35.987
System context matters.

00:01:35.987 --> 00:01:47.209
A personal laptop, managed enterprise Mac, shared lab workstation, developer machine, or Apple Silicon device may expose different artifacts and collection constraints.

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:57.954
macOS privacy controls, system integrity protections, encryption, mobile device management, and user permissions all affect what can be collected and interpreted.

00:01:57.954 --> 00:02:03.923
The folder structure is the starting point, not the whole investigation.
